@page "/docs"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.AllowAnonymous]
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject IJSRuntime Js

<PageTitle>User docs — CalCrony</PageTitle>

<p class="meta-mono text-uppercase mb-2">User documentation</p>
<h1>Using CalCrony</h1>
<p class="lead text-body-secondary" style="max-width:58ch">
    Most things work from both Discord and this web app, and a few live on one side only — see
    Features in depth for which.
    Sign in with Discord and you'll see exactly the servers you share with the bot.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">First steps for server admins</h2>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Right after inviting the bot, run these two commands once — they shape everything else:
</p>
<ol class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <code>/settings server-timezone</code> — pick your zone from the command's suggestions
        (start typing a city, e.g. <code>America/Chicago</code>). Natural-language times
        ("tomorrow 6pm") are parsed in this zone; until it's set, the server runs on <b>UTC</b>,
        so local wall-clock times can land hours off or be rejected as "in the past". (Members
        can also set a personal <code>/settings timezone</code>, which wins over the server's
        for their own inputs.)
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <code>/settings default-channel</code> pointing at your events channel. Events, polls,
        and reminders created from this web app post their embeds there — <b>web creation is
        blocked until it's set</b>. Bot commands aren't affected (they post where they're run).
    </li>
</ol>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Optional third step: <code>/settings native-events enabled:true</code> mirrors events into the server's
    built-in Events tab (the bot must hold <b>Manage Events</b>; existing events mirror when next
    edited). Check everything anytime with <code>/settings view</code> or from the server's
    settings page here.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">Set up your interest roles</h2>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Several features point at Discord roles, so a few minutes in <b>Server Settings → Roles</b>
    pays off. Two patterns matter, and they behave very differently:
</p>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Interest roles</b> — standing groups people join once and keep, e.g.
        <code>@@dnd</code>, <code>@@movie-night</code>, <code>@@raiders</code>. Let members
        self-assign them (Discord's onboarding prompts or a roles channel work great). These are
        what you point <i>planning</i> features at:
        <code>/availability role role:@@dnd</code> shows the whole group's free/busy grid before
        you pick a session time, and <code>/notify mention:@@dnd</code> pings the group before an
        event starts.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Attendee roles</b> — temporary, per-event badges CalCrony manages for you, e.g.
        <code>@@session-players</code>. Create the role empty and pass it at
        <code>/create attendee-role:</code> — everyone who RSVPs <b>Going</b> gets it
        automatically, switching away or removing the RSVP removes it (unless, with multiple RSVPs
        on, another option they still hold grants the same role), and the whole role empties when
        the event ends. Perfect for pinging only confirmed attendees or temporarily unlocking a
        planning channel.
        <br />
        Each RSVP option can hand out a <i>different</i> role: mention one inside
        <code>rsvp-options</code> and whoever picks that option gets that role. So
        <code>rsvp-options:"🛡️ Tank x2 @@tank, 💚 Healer x2 @@healer, ⚔️ DPS x6 @@dps"</code>
        sorts a raid into three roles from one event, and switching from Tank to Healer swaps
        the roles over automatically (with multiple RSVPs on, a member holding both seats holds
        both roles).
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Restricting signup by role</b> — the third use of interest roles: only their holders
        may RSVP. <code>/create restrict-to:@@raiders</code> limits every option to
        <code>@@raiders</code> (up to five roles, any one of them will do), and inside
        <code>rsvp-options</code> the word <code>only:</code> limits a single option —
        <code>rsvp-options:"🛡️ Tank x2 @@tank only: @@raiders, ❌ Out"</code> grants
        <code>@@tank</code> to whoever picks Tank <i>and</i> lets only <code>@@raiders</code> pick
        it (the grant mention goes before <code>only:</code>, the restriction after).
        <code>/poll create restrict-to:</code> does the same for voting, and
        <code>/edit restrict-to:</code> / <code>clear-restriction:true</code> change or remove one.
        The event creator and server managers always pass. A restriction only gates entry: losing
        the role later never costs anyone their seat, and a role that gets deleted simply stops
        counting. Restrictions are set from Discord only — the web can remove one, not add one.
    </li>
</ul>
<Callout Variant="CalloutVariant.Caution">
    Don't use a standing interest role as an <code>attendee-role</code>: CalCrony removes the
    attendee role from everyone when the event ends, which would wipe your group's membership.
    Keep the two kinds separate — interest roles for reach, attendee roles for attendance.
    (The bot needs <b>Manage Roles</b> — the current invite link grants it — and you must drag
    roles the bot manages <b>below the bot's own role</b> in the role list yourself; role
    ordering is something no invite can set.)
</Callout>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">Worked example: weekly D&amp;D night</h2>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    From zero to a self-running game night:
</p>
<ol class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-1">Create two roles: <code>@@dnd</code> (interest — let players self-assign) and
        <code>@@session-players</code> (empty; CalCrony will manage it).</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/availability role role:@@dnd when:"friday 7pm" duration:180</code> —
        check the group's calendars before committing to a slot (players link theirs once with
        <code>/calendar connect</code>).</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/create title:"D&amp;D Night" when:"friday 7pm" duration:180
        repeat:weekly attendee-role:@@session-players thread:true</code> — a recurring event with
        RSVP buttons, a discussion thread that Going RSVPers join automatically, and an
        auto-managed attendee role.</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/notify event:"D&amp;D Night" minutes-before:60 mention:@@dnd</code> —
        the whole interest group gets a one-hour heads-up; add a second one mentioning
        <code>@@session-players</code> at 10 minutes for just the confirmed table.</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/template save name:"Game Night" event:"D&amp;D Night"</code> — next
        campaign or a one-shot, start from <code>/create template:"Game Night"</code> and only
        pick a time.</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Skip a holiday week with <code>/series skip</code>; the next occurrence posts
        itself when each session ends. <code>/link</code> gives everyone a calendar-app
        subscription that carries the whole schedule.</li>
</ol>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    The same recipe fits movie nights, raid schedules, or book clubs — swap the roles and the
    repeat rule.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2" id="features">Features in depth</h2>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Every feature: where it lives, the Discord permission it needs, and what is on by default.
    Most work from Discord and from this web app; a few live on one side only — attendee roles,
    signup restrictions and live lists are set in Discord; themes, the activity log and the CSV
    export are web-only — and each entry says which. Anything <b>opt-in</b> stays off until someone turns it on: a server
    manager for server-wide switches (native events, the public calendar), the event's creator or
    a manager for per-event ones (multiple RSVPs), and only you for DM reminders. The three
    permissions mentioned below — <b>Manage Events</b>, <b>Manage Roles</b>, <b>Create Public
    Threads</b> — are on the current invite link; a server that added the bot earlier must
    re-invite it or grant them to the bot's role.
</p>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Events &amp; RSVPs</h3>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Events</b> — <code>/create</code> or the web form; times are plain English ("tomorrow
        6pm", "in 5 hours") parsed in your personal timezone, else the server's. The embed carries
        the time as a Discord timestamp, so everyone sees it in their own zone. Only the creator or
        a server manager can edit or delete. Web-created events post to the server's default
        channel (<code>/settings default-channel</code>).
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>RSVP buttons and custom options</b> — ✅ Going / ❌ Not going / 🤔 Maybe by default,
        with live attendee lists on the embed and the event page. Define your own with
        <code>rsvp-options:"⚔️ Raider x10, 🛡️ Standby, ❌ Out"</code> or the form's option editor:
        any emoji and label, up to ten. Exactly one option is the <i>attending</i> one — the first,
        or the one marked with a trailing <code>*</code> (the form's radio) — and it is what
        roles, threads, availability and the waitlist key on. On <code>/edit</code>,
        <code>rsvp-options</code> matches existing options by label so their RSVPs survive, an
        option that has RSVPs can't be removed, and each entry you send replaces that option's
        role and restriction — so restate them; the web form's editor keeps them as they were.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Attendee limits, the waitlist, and closing early</b> — <code>attendee-limit:6</code>
        caps the attending option (or <code>xN</code> on any option, or the form's capacity box).
        RSVPs past the cap on the attending option queue on a waitlist shown on the embed and the
        event page; when a spot frees, the first in line is seated and pinged automatically. A
        full non-attending option simply refuses. <code>rsvp-close:"2h before"</code> (or an
        absolute time), or the form's "RSVPs close" field, freezes RSVP changes before start;
        raising a cap seats the waitlist in order, and a cap can't go below the seats already taken.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Multiple RSVPs per member</b> (<b>opt-in</b> per event) — <code>multi-rsvp:true</code>
        on <code>/create</code>, <code>multi-rsvp:true</code> or <code>multi-rsvp:false</code> on
        <code>/edit</code>, or the form's
        "Allow more than one RSVP per member" box. Members can hold several options at once (Tank
        <i>and</i> Healer, dinner <i>and</i> movie): clicking another button adds it, clicking a
        chosen one again removes it, and only the attending option keeps a waitlist. No option is
        exclusive — with the default set a member could hold Going <i>and</i> Not going — so choose
        options that combine sensibly. Turning it back off is refused while anyone holds more than
        one RSVP.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Event threads</b> — <code>thread:true</code> or the form's "Create a discussion thread"
        box opens a thread on the event message; members seated on the attending option (Going by
        default) are added automatically so they get
        its notifications, and it archives when the event ends, is deleted, skipped, or cancelled —
        history stays readable. Needs <b>Create Public Threads</b>.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Event templates</b> — <code>/template save</code> (or Save as template on an event page)
        keeps an event's content, reminders and repeat rule under a name; start from it with
        <code>/create template:</code> or the form's picker. Up to 25 per server; the creator or a
        manager edits or deletes one, and reminder lists are edited on the Templates tab here.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Discord native events</b> (<b>opt-in</b> per server) — <code>/settings native-events
        enabled:true</code>, or the toggle on the server settings page here, mirrors events into
        the server's built-in Events tab through their whole
        lifecycle (created, updated, deleted, active at start, completed at end). The native
        description points at the RSVP embed so "Interested" isn't mistaken for an RSVP. Needs
        <b>Manage Events</b>; existing events mirror when next edited.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Schedules</h3>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Recurring events</b> — every N days, weeks, months (by date or nth weekday, "3rd
        Friday") or years; weekly schedules can tick a set of days (Tue + Thu, or the Weekdays
        preset) and still repeat every N weeks. The schedule is anchored on the first occurrence
        with timezone-aware math, so 8pm stays 8pm across DST. When one occurrence ends or is
        skipped, the next posts itself; end by date or count, or never. Edits and reminders ask
        "this occurrence or the whole series?"; <code>/series edit</code> changes the rule or end
        condition afterwards, and editing an ended series revives it. On the web: pick the schedule
        on the event form (repeat controls with live previews), and skip, stop, or change the rule
        from the event page.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Live list</b> — <code>/livelist create</code> posts an upcoming-events embed the bot
        keeps current: it rewrites when events are created, edited, deleted, or RSVPed, when an
        occurrence rolls, and at bot startup, debounced so busy servers don't hammer Discord. One
        per channel, managers only; deleting the message removes the list.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Reminders &amp; calendars</h3>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Scheduled pings and reminders</b> — <code>/notify</code>, or <b>Scheduled notifications</b>
        on an event page here, adds up to five pings before an event starts (with an optional
        message and mention), and every event announces its start; <code>/remind</code>, or the
        <b>Remind me</b> form on a server's events page, is a one-off reminder in a channel. All of
        it goes through a crash-safe outbox, so a restart never loses a ping.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>DM reminders</b> (<b>opt-in</b> per user, off by default) — <code>/settings dm-reminders
        enabled:true</code> or My settings here: reminders and start announcements for events
        you're attending also arrive by DM. The bot offers the toggle exactly once, as an ephemeral
        prompt after your first attending RSVP made with a Discord button (a web RSVP never
        prompts — use My settings here) — never an unsolicited DM — and if your DMs are
        closed to it, it switches the setting off instead of retrying. Servers and event creators
        can never turn it on for you.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>ICS subscribe feed</b> — <code>/link</code> (or the server settings page) gives a
        tokenized URL for Google, Apple, and Outlook calendars; the calendar is named after the
        server ("CalCrony · Your Server"). Repeating events carry their full schedule (RRULE), so
        subscribers see every future occurrence. Each entry's description names its channel and
        links to the event's web page and to the RSVP embed in Discord — except recurring series,
        which link to the server's events page instead, since their live occurrence rotates.
        Calendar apps refresh on their own schedule (see Good to know below).
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Public web calendar</b> (<b>opt-in</b> per server, managers) — <code>/settings
        public-calendar mode:on</code> or Server settings here: a login-free month view of the server's
        events behind an unguessable link, including upcoming occurrences of repeating ones, with
        entries that jump to the Discord message. It shows titles, start times, durations,
        locations, and channel names only — never descriptions, RSVPs, or member names — and asks
        search engines not to index it. <code>mode:new-link</code> retires a leaked link;
        <code>mode:off</code> closes it.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Google Calendar availability</b> — link your calendar with <code>/calendar connect</code>
        or under My settings (free/busy scope only: CalCrony never sees event titles or details,
        and tokens are encrypted at rest). Then <code>/availability role</code> or
        <code>/availability event</code> — and the grid on every event page — show who's free,
        Scheduling-Assistant style. Read-only: it never blocks creating or RSVPing.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Polls</h3>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Polls and time polls</b> — <code>/poll create</code> or the Polls tab: up to ten
        options, single or multi vote, anonymous mode, voter-added options (➕ opens a modal), a
        natural-language close deadline with automatic closing, and live bar-graph results on the
        embed. <code>/poll time</code> pits candidate times against each other; voters pick every
        time they can make, and a closed time poll's winner converts into a real event with one
        click (<code>/poll convert</code> or the button here).
    </li>
</ul>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Roles &amp; access</h3>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Attendee roles</b> — an existing role given to whoever is seated on an option and taken
        back when they leave it, or from everyone when the event ends, is deleted, skipped, or
        cancelled. Event-wide with <code>attendee-role:</code>, or
        per option by mentioning the role inside <code>rsvp-options</code> (see Set up your interest
        roles above for the Tank/Healer/DPS pattern). Grants land within about 15 seconds; with
        multiple RSVPs on, a member seated on two options holds both roles until their last seat
        carrying one goes. Roles are picked in Discord only — the event page lists them. Needs
        <b>Manage Roles</b>, with the roles below the bot's own.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Role-restricted signup</b> — <code>restrict-to:@@raiders</code> limits every option (or
        a poll's votes) to holders of the named roles, up to five; <code>only: @@role</code> inside
        an option limits just that one. In Discord the bot checks your roles live. Here the API
        answers from role snapshots the bot keeps and <b>fails closed</b>: if it can't confirm them
        (no snapshot yet, or the bot has been offline for more than half an hour) it asks you to
        RSVP or vote from Discord. Restricted options show a 🔒 chip naming the roles and stay
        clickable. The creator and managers always pass; a restriction only gates entry, so losing
        the role later never costs a seat; setting one happens in Discord, and the edit form here
        can only remove one.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3 class="h6 mt-3">Web app &amp; admin</h3>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Everything from the browser</b> — sign in with Discord (identify and server list only,
        never your messages) and create, edit, and delete events, RSVP, run polls, manage templates,
        notifications, and server and personal settings; embeds post, update, and disappear in
        Discord within about 15 seconds. Pick one of five interface themes, each with a dark and a
        light face, under My settings — it follows your account across devices.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Activity log &amp; CSV export</b> (managers) — the Activity tab shows every
        user-initiated management action (events, series, polls, templates, settings, live lists),
        who did it and from where (Discord or web), filterable by action and member, kept for 90
        days by default (self-hosters set <code>Retention__ActionLogDays</code>). Its <b>Export
        events (CSV)</b> button downloads every event with its RSVPs — one row
        per RSVP, custom options and waitlist status included. Free for every server; there is no
        premium tier.
    </li>
</ul>

